[music]

00:03:56.892 --> 00:04:04.194 align:start position:0%
Almost every piece of land on Earth
belongs to someone. Borders cut through

00:04:01.694 --> 00:04:09.494 align:start position:0%
mountains, grasslands, [music] forests,
and rivers, but the oceans are

00:04:06.294 --> 00:04:16.094 align:start position:0%
different. Most remain outside national
boundaries open to ships from almost

00:04:12.294 --> 00:04:21.194 align:start position:0%
anywhere. That openness was by choice.
As naval technology improved during the

00:04:18.694 --> 00:04:26.594 align:start position:0%
20th century, the leading powers [music]
gained the ability to patrol distant

00:04:23.394 --> 00:04:30.694 align:start position:0%
waters and enforce claims far from their
shores. [music] A country might

00:04:28.694 --> 00:04:35.794 align:start position:0%
reasonably want to protect its offshore
resources and defend its coastline, but

00:04:33.694 --> 00:04:40.794 align:start position:0%
allowing governments to restrict every
passage near their shores would have

00:04:38.094 --> 00:04:45.294 align:start position:0%
made international trade exceedingly
difficult. [music] The solution was a

00:04:42.894 --> 00:04:50.594 align:start position:0%
compromise between the economic rights
of coastal states and the navigational

00:04:47.894 --> 00:04:53.494 align:start position:0%
freedoms of everyone else.
For decades, [music]

00:04:51.594 --> 00:04:58.194 align:start position:0%
that arrangement served the United
States particularly well. American

00:04:56.094 --> 00:05:03.694 align:start position:0%
merchant ships could reach markets
around the world while the US Navy could

00:05:00.994 --> 00:05:08.494 align:start position:0%
operate across distant waters without
constantly seeking permission from

00:05:05.894 --> 00:05:13.294 align:start position:0%
foreign governments. Washington [music]
never ratified the UN Convention on the

00:05:10.794 --> 00:05:17.794 align:start position:0%
Law of the Sea, partly because of
objections to its seabed [music] mining

00:05:15.694 --> 00:05:22.194 align:start position:0%
provisions, but it nevertheless
supported many of the convention's

00:05:20.094 --> 00:05:28.194 align:start position:0%
principles. [music] Keeping the oceans
open was ultimately more valuable than

00:05:25.294 --> 00:05:33.394 align:start position:0%
trying to control every choke point.
However, the system had a built-in flaw.

00:05:31.094 --> 00:05:37.494 align:start position:0%
International tribunals can determine
legal rights, but they cannot enforce

00:05:35.394 --> 00:05:42.494 align:start position:0%
their judgments through force. [music]
The UN Security Council could impose

00:05:39.994 --> 00:05:45.794 align:start position:0%
sanctions or authorize action to protect
shipping, [music]

00:05:43.194 --> 00:05:51.494 align:start position:0%
except that China, Russia, and the
United States can each block such

00:05:48.594 --> 00:05:56.394 align:start position:0%
decisions with a veto. That leaves
enforcement largely in the hands of

00:05:53.794 --> 00:06:01.094 align:start position:0%
individual governments, including those
accused of violating the rules.

00:05:59.094 --> 00:06:06.795 align:start position:0%
China has [music] spent much of the past
20 years exploiting this flaw. Beijing

00:06:04.294 --> 00:06:11.295 align:start position:0%
claims maritime rights across waters
also claimed by neighboring countries,

00:06:09.195 --> 00:06:17.395 align:start position:0%
including the Philippines. [music] In
2016, an international tribunal rejected

00:06:14.995 --> 00:06:21.595 align:start position:0%
China's claims beyond what the law of
the sea permits.

00:06:19.295 --> 00:06:26.495 align:start position:0%
Beijing, however, refused to accept the
ruling and continued strengthening its

00:06:23.995 --> 00:06:30.995 align:start position:0%
presence through artificial islands and
naval deployments. [music] For the

00:06:28.495 --> 00:06:36.195 align:start position:0%
Philippines, this created a dangerous
reality. International law recognized

00:06:34.195 --> 00:06:40.864 align:start position:0%
Philippine maritime rights, but
exercising them was another matter.

00:06:38.595 --> 00:06:44.795 align:start position:0%
Chinese vessels could obstruct fishing
[music] boats, disrupt resupply

00:06:42.895 --> 00:06:49.995 align:start position:0%
missions, and make operating in
contested waters increasingly risky.

00:06:48.095 --> 00:06:55.995 align:start position:0%
Manila could appeal to international
institutions and seek support from its

00:06:52.895 --> 00:07:00.895 align:start position:0%
allies, but the tribunal itself had no
means of forcing Beijing to comply. And

00:06:58.695 --> 00:07:04.795 align:start position:0%
this is where the distinction between
legal rights and physical control

00:07:03.095 --> 00:07:09.495 align:start position:0%
becomes [music] crucial.
Beijing does not need the international

00:07:06.995 --> 00:07:13.895 align:start position:0%
community to recognize the South China
Sea [music] as its territory. By

00:07:11.695 --> 00:07:19.195 align:start position:0%
maintaining a powerful military and
coast guard presence, Beijing can

00:07:16.095 --> 00:07:24.195 align:start position:0%
influence who operates in disputed areas
and under what conditions. Over time,

00:07:22.295 --> 00:07:29.695 align:start position:0%
that practical influence can become
increasingly difficult to reverse, even

00:07:26.895 --> 00:07:33.795 align:start position:0%
if the underlying claims remain legally
contested.

00:07:31.195 --> 00:07:38.995 align:start position:0%
Washington has repeatedly pushed back
against this policy by conducting

00:07:36.295 --> 00:07:43.095 align:start position:0%
freedom of navigation operations.
America's [music]

00:07:39.795 --> 00:07:48.095 align:start position:0%
concern is understandable. If powerful
coastal states can decide which foreign

00:07:45.795 --> 00:07:52.695 align:start position:0%
vessels are allowed to operate in
surrounding waters, American naval

00:07:50.495 --> 00:07:57.895 align:start position:0%
mobility would become increasingly
vulnerable to political restrictions.

00:07:54.880 --> 00:08:02.795 align:start position:0%
[music] And since the US Navy underpins
American power projection, restricting

00:08:00.395 --> 00:08:06.895 align:start position:0%
its movement would thereby diminish
Washington's influence.

00:08:05.195 --> 00:08:12.297 align:start position:0%
On the flip side, though, China's
position is also understandable. It

00:08:09.697 --> 00:08:16.497 align:start position:0%
depends enormously on international
shipping. Its factories [music]

00:08:13.997 --> 00:08:21.097 align:start position:0%
require raw materials from distant
suppliers, while its [music] exports

00:08:18.597 --> 00:08:27.197 align:start position:0%
travel through waters controlled or
influenced by others. Beijing therefore

00:08:24.197 --> 00:08:31.297 align:start position:0%
benefits from the open seas even as it
seeks greater control over certain

00:08:29.897 --> 00:08:36.997 align:start position:0%
regions.
This then exposes a predicament at the

00:08:33.897 --> 00:08:41.297 align:start position:0%
heart of the maritime order. Governments
may defend international rules for

00:08:38.997 --> 00:08:45.997 align:start position:0%
decades only to challenge them when
those same rules stand in the way of

00:08:43.697 --> 00:08:51.697 align:start position:0%
something they consider strategically
essential. And China [music] is hardly

00:08:48.497 --> 00:08:57.297 align:start position:0%
alone. Iran, Russia, Turkey, Britain,
Canada, Norway, and Australia have all

00:08:54.797 --> 00:09:02.097 align:start position:0%
challenged aspects of the maritime order
in pursuit of their own interests.

00:08:59.726 --> 00:09:07.697 align:start position:0%
[music] But the consequences are not
equal. The laws of the sea are only

00:09:04.997 --> 00:09:12.797 align:start position:0%
effective when powerful states find it
convenient to obey them. For everyone

00:09:10.297 --> 00:09:17.097 align:start position:0%
else, the rules are merely polite
suggestions.

00:09:14.397 --> 00:09:21.897 align:start position:0%
