00:09:50.397 --> 00:09:57.897 align:start position:0%
Up to this point, the pressure has been
on shipping lanes and choke points, but

00:09:54.997 --> 00:10:01.897 align:start position:0%
the partition of the oceans is widening.
Countries are interested [music] not

00:09:59.997 --> 00:10:07.997 align:start position:0%
only in controlling what moves on the
surface, but also what lies underneath.

00:10:05.797 --> 00:10:13.598 align:start position:0%
Huge areas of the Pacific and Indian
Oceans contain [music] polymetallic

00:10:10.298 --> 00:10:17.998 align:start position:0%
nodules rich in valuable minerals. Among
the biggest deposits are the

00:10:15.298 --> 00:10:23.298 align:start position:0%
Clarion-Clipperton zone between Hawaii
and Mexico, the central Indian Ocean

00:10:20.698 --> 00:10:28.198 align:start position:0%
basin, and [music] the Penrhyn basin in
the South Pacific. The International

00:10:25.598 --> 00:10:32.698 align:start position:0%
Seabed Authority has already issued
dozens of permits allowing governments

00:10:30.798 --> 00:10:37.598 align:start position:0%
and companies [music] to explore these
areas, map deposits, and test extraction

00:10:36.298 --> 00:10:42.698 align:start position:0%
methods.
The only thing preventing a free-for-all

00:10:39.598 --> 00:10:47.098 align:start position:0%
mining frenzy is that lawmakers cannot
seem to agree on environmental

00:10:44.698 --> 00:10:51.498 align:start position:0%
safeguards. Much of the deep ocean
remains poorly understood and

00:10:49.198 --> 00:10:56.598 align:start position:0%
large-scale mining could damage
ecosystems that scientists have barely

00:10:54.298 --> 00:11:01.898 align:start position:0%
begun to study. But Washington is
unwilling to wait. Since the United

00:10:58.898 --> 00:11:06.998 align:start position:0%
States never joined the UN Convention on
the Law of the Sea, it is [music]

00:11:03.598 --> 00:11:09.298 align:start position:0%
technically not bound by it. As such,
the Trump administration has

00:11:08.598 --> 00:11:13.998 align:start position:0%
fast-tracked [music]
permits for deep-sea mining in the

00:11:11.198 --> 00:11:20.298 align:start position:0%
Clarion-Clipperton zone and is opening
the seabed near American Samoa for

00:11:16.798 --> 00:11:24.898 align:start position:0%
exploration and commercial extraction.
Washington may see that as a way to

00:11:22.598 --> 00:11:31.298 align:start position:0%
secure resources outside China's
control, but other powers see something

00:11:28.098 --> 00:11:36.198 align:start position:0%
else, a precedent. China, India, and
Russia have all signed onto

00:11:33.398 --> 00:11:41.198 align:start position:0%
international rules governing seabed
extraction. [music] If the United States

00:11:38.598 --> 00:11:46.098 align:start position:0%
begins mining independently of those
rules, Beijing and Moscow [music] can

00:11:43.798 --> 00:11:51.338 align:start position:0%
point to Washington the next time they
want an exception or seek alternative

00:11:48.698 --> 00:11:55.098 align:start position:0%
arrangements of their own. So American
[music]

00:11:51.898 --> 00:12:00.098 align:start position:0%
unilateralism gives competitors a legit
justification to disregard maritime

00:11:57.598 --> 00:12:03.298 align:start position:0%
rules when it suits them.
And that may [music] be the most

00:12:01.498 --> 00:12:08.598 align:start position:0%
important element of the entire
partition. Ocean governance is unlikely

00:12:06.598 --> 00:12:13.399 align:start position:0%
to collapse because every country
suddenly tears up the same treaty. It is

00:12:11.598 --> 00:12:18.999 align:start position:0%
more [music] likely to die through a
succession of smaller exceptions. One

00:12:16.299 --> 00:12:24.599 align:start position:0%
country charges ships, another restricts
passage, a third mines a disputed

00:12:21.899 --> 00:12:28.199 align:start position:0%
seabed, and some just ignore tribunal
rulings.

00:12:26.199 --> 00:12:32.599 align:start position:0%
Each precedent makes the next one
slightly easier to defend, [music] and

00:12:30.399 --> 00:12:37.699 align:start position:0%
once that becomes the norm, it will not
stop with shipping lanes or minerals.

00:12:35.599 --> 00:12:42.199 align:start position:0%
Governments could become more aggressive
over fishing grounds, subsea cables,

00:12:40.463 --> 00:12:48.599 align:start position:0%
[music] and strategically located
islands. The waters surrounding Taiwan,

00:12:45.499 --> 00:12:52.899 align:start position:0%
Svalbard, Cyprus, and even Greenland
could all become fair play.

00:12:50.899 --> 00:12:56.699 align:start position:0%
The eventual consequences could be
world-changing. [music]

00:12:54.399 --> 00:13:01.199 align:start position:0%
Ships forced to take longer routes or
pay more fees would make trade more

00:12:58.819 --> 00:13:05.599 align:start position:0%
[music] expensive. Supply chains would
become less reliable, while governments

00:13:03.799 --> 00:13:11.599 align:start position:0%
controlling choke points could gain
greater leverage over food, energy, and

00:13:08.599 --> 00:13:15.999 align:start position:0%
strategic resources. Every disputed
island or seabed could become a flash

00:13:14.199 --> 00:13:21.099 align:start position:0%
point on its own.
That in turn would put a premium on

00:13:18.299 --> 00:13:26.499 align:start position:0%
naval power. Countries hoping to defend
or enforce maritime claims would need

00:13:23.799 --> 00:13:31.799 align:start position:0%
larger and more capable fleets. That
means more surface warships and a

00:13:28.899 --> 00:13:36.599 align:start position:0%
greater naval industrial capacity. As
the partition of the oceans gathers

00:13:34.099 --> 00:13:41.299 align:start position:0%
momentum, naval power would once again
become one of the clearest ways to turn

00:13:38.999 --> 00:13:44.599 align:start position:0%
a claim on paper into control [music]
at sea.

00:13:42.699 --> 00:13:50.099 align:start position:0%
We've seen this story before. The
conquest and division of land rewarded

00:13:47.299 --> 00:13:54.299 align:start position:0%
countries that arrived first or were
strong enough to defend what they

00:13:51.799 --> 00:14:00.099 align:start position:0%
claimed. [music] Borders then shifted
repeatedly as empires rose and fell, and

00:13:57.799 --> 00:14:04.099 align:start position:0%
it took centuries of war and state
building for most of those boundaries to

00:14:02.599 --> 00:14:08.999 align:start position:0%
stabilize. [music]
The oceans escaped much of that history

00:14:06.599 --> 00:14:13.199 align:start position:0%
because governments eventually decided
they gained more [music] from keeping

00:14:10.799 --> 00:14:18.201 align:start position:0%
them open than from fighting over every
piece of water. If that understanding

00:14:15.439 --> 00:14:23.001 align:start position:0%
[music] breaks down, the law of the sea
may give way to a much older rule, the

00:14:20.801 --> 00:14:27.501 align:start position:0%
law of the jungle.
I've been your host Shirvan from Caspian

00:14:24.801 --> 00:14:32.601 align:start position:0%
Report. If you approve of what we do,
please leave a comment, hit the like

00:14:29.501 --> 00:14:37.001 align:start position:0%
